### Finding: discharge_location missingness is structural

27.44% of admissions lack `discharge_location`. Missingness is
concentrated in observation-type admissions (EU OBSERVATION 98.97%,
AMBULATORY OBSERVATION 98.68%, DIRECT OBSERVATION 96.98%) and is
below 1% for the other admission types shown (e.g. DIRECT EMER. 0.26%,
SURGICAL SAME DAY ADMISSION 0.20%). These three types account for
about 99.5% of all missing values. This indicates the gaps are
systematic rather than random; likely cause not yet verified.
Implication: excluding rows with missing discharge_location would
remove most observation stays and could bias downstream analyses.

### Finding: disguised missing values in race

`race` has 0.0% null values but 13,870 admissions (2.54%) are
labelled UNKNOWN, so a null-only check understates incompleteness.
(OTHER, 19,788 admissions, 3.62%, is a valid but uninformative
category.) Counts are per admission, not per patient.
Implication: completeness checks should include placeholder values,
not just nulls.

Among the 4,494 patients with differing broad groups, 3,199 (71%)
involve the uninformative category OTHER (OTHER/WHITE 2,235;
BLACK/OTHER 629; HISPANIC/OTHER 335). Combinations not involving
OTHER, which are the clearest candidates for genuine inconsistency,
are HISPANIC/WHITE 263, BLACK/WHITE 187, and BLACK/HISPANIC 134
(584 patients, about 0.6% of 96,925 comparable patients). Top six
combinations shown; remaining combinations not listed.
